  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Arizona total $26.5B for FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $18.5B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $6.2B. Procurement activity reached 17,322 awards with an average award size of $1.5M, and obligations are up 13.8% year over year. Top vendors include RAYTHEON COMPANY with $8.5B, TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP with $4.7B, and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO with $4.5B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction each account for $6.5B of obligations. Historical annual obligations show FY21 at $16.5B, FY22 at $15.3B, FY23 at $19.8B, FY24 at $18B, FY25 at $23.3B, and FY26 at $26.5B. This mix of defense, homeland security, construction, and health insurers drives the current-year totals, which are fiscal year to date and therefore partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did federal contract spending change year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations are up 13.8% year over year. Annual totals climbed from $23.3B in FY25 to $26.5B in FY26. Award count stands at 17,322 with an average award size of $1.5M, reflecting the scale of the increase."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries drive Arizona federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads with $18.5B in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $6.2B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction each account for $6.5B, and Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers account for $4.7B. Top vendors include RAYTHEON COMPANY at $8.5B and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $4.7B."
      }
    ]
  },
